<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Derbent Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1806 CE · capital Derbent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived khanate based in the ancient fortress city of Derbent, the Caspian gateway between the North Caucasus and Transcaucasia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah's</a> death in 1747, the Persian governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-husayn-khan-of-derbent">Muhammad Husayn Khan</a>, ruled the walled city and its hinterland as an independent khan. Derbent's Sasanian walls and citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naryn-kala">Naryn-Kala</a> still controlled the narrow coastal passage between the mountains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khanate was small but strategically vital, drawing income from customs on the coastal route and from the trade of its port. Its population was mixed, including Azerbaijani, Persian, Tat-speaking Jewish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezgins">Lezgin</a> communities. Rivalry with neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kaitag-utsmiyate">Kaitag</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Quba</a> weakened it, and in 1765 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fatali Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quba-khanate">Quba Khanate</a> seized the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> remained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Quba's</a> rulers until Russian troops took it in 1796, briefly, and again in 1806, when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed it permanently. The city's walls and citadel are now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/derbent-khanate.jpg" alt="Derbent Khanate" width="320" height="394"><figcaption>Image: AoifeIves &amp;amp; Khotab · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Derbent_Khanate_in_the_18th_Century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Derbent</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad Husayn Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani; Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Derbent</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=derbent-khanate" aria-label="Derbent Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5853 681 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Derbent Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6173 937L6298 1144M6173 937L6186 950M6173 937L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6186 950h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6173 937h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6173 937h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Derbent. <a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=derbent-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
